// 2011 GS1 (Prelims) Set A — Transcribed from "E:\UPSC question paper\2011\question and answer prilims 2011.docx"
// Contains question, choices (A, B, C, D), answer, explanation, and upsc review points
export const paper = { year: 2011, name: 'GS1', set: 'A' };

export const questions = [
{"no":1,"text":"In the Union Budget 2011–12, full exemption from basic customs duty was extended to bio-based asphalt (bioasphalt). What is the importance of this material?\nUnlike traditional asphalt, bio-asphalt is not based on fossil fuels.\nBio-asphalt can be made from non-renewable resources.\nBio-asphalt can be made from organic waste materials.\nIt is eco-friendly to use bio-asphalt for road surfacing.\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:","A":"1, 2 and 3 only","B":"1, 3 and 4 only","C":"2 and 4 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Environmental Technology","tags":["Bioasphalt","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Bio-asphalt is produced using biological/renewable feedstocks rather than relying on conventional fossil-fuel-derived materials.\nOrganic waste materials can be used as inputs for producing bio-asphalt.\nIts use can reduce dependence on conventional petroleum-based asphalt.\nTherefore, it can provide an environmentally friendly alternative for road construction.\nStatement 2 is incorrect because the source distinguishes bio-based material from non-renewable resources.","revisionPoints":"Bio-asphalt → bio-based alternative to conventional asphalt.\nCan use organic waste materials.\nReduces dependence on fossil-fuel-based materials.\nRelevant to sustainable infrastructure and waste utilisation."},
{"no":2,"text":"Consider the following:\nCarbon dioxide\nOxides of nitrogen\nOxides of sulphur\nWhich of the above are emissions from coal combustion at thermal power plants?","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Pollution","tags":["Coal Combustion","Thermal Power Plants","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Burning coal produces carbon dioxide (CO₂) as a major greenhouse-gas emission.\nHigh-temperature combustion can produce nitrogen oxides (NOx).\nSulphur present in coal can lead to sulphur oxides (SOx) during combustion.\nThese emissions have implications for climate change and air pollution.","revisionPoints":"Coal combustion → CO₂ emissions.\nNOx → contributes to air pollution and atmospheric chemical reactions.\nSOx → associated with acid rain and respiratory pollution.\nThermal power plants therefore require emission-control technologies."},
{"no":3,"text":"Satellites used for telecommunication relay are kept in a geostationary orbit. A satellite is said to be in such an orbit when:\nThe orbit is geosynchronous.\nThe orbit is circular.\nThe orbit lies in the plane of the Earth's equator.\nThe orbit is at an altitude of 22,236 km.\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:","A":"1, 2 and 3 only","B":"1, 3 and 4 only","C":"2 and 4 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Space","tags":["Geostationary Orbit","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"A geostationary satellite has an orbital period equal to the Earth's rotational period, making it geosynchronous.\nIts orbit must be circular.\nIt must lie in the equatorial plane.\nThe commonly cited altitude is about 35,786 km above the equator, so statement 4 is incorrect.","revisionPoints":"Geostationary orbit → special type of geosynchronous orbit.\nOrbit is circular and equatorial.\nOrbital period ≈ 24 hours.\nSatellite appears stationary relative to a point on the Earth's equator."},
{"no":4,"text":"India has experienced persistent and high food inflation. What could be the reasons?\nDue to a gradual switchover to commercial crops, the area under food grains has steadily decreased by about 30% in the last five years.\nIncreasing incomes have changed consumption patterns.\nThe food supply chain has structural constraints.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Food Inflation","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Rising incomes can increase demand for high-value foods, changing the composition of food demand.\nStructural problems in storage, transportation, distribution and marketing can contribute to food-price pressures.\nThe claim of a 30% decline in food-grain cultivation area is not supported by the source.\nTherefore, statements 2 and 3 are correct.","revisionPoints":"Food inflation can arise from demand-side and supply-side factors.\nRising incomes can alter consumption patterns.\nSupply-chain bottlenecks can increase post-harvest losses and prices.\nAgricultural productivity and distribution infrastructure affect food prices."},
{"no":5,"text":"Scientists can determine the arrangement or relative positions of genes or DNA sequences on a chromosome. How does this knowledge benefit us?\nIt is possible to know the pedigree of livestock.\nIt is possible to understand the causes of all human diseases.\nIt is possible to develop disease-resistant animal breeds.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biotechnology","tags":["Gene Mapping","DNA Sequencing","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Mapping genes can help identify genetic relationships and assist in determining livestock pedigree.\nGenetic information can be used in selective breeding programmes.\nIdentification of genes associated with disease resistance can support development of disease-resistant animal breeds.\nIt would be incorrect to say that this knowledge allows us to understand the causes of all human diseases.","revisionPoints":"Gene mapping → determines relative positions of genes/DNA sequences.\nUseful in genetic research and breeding programmes.\nCan support selective breeding for desirable traits.\nNot all diseases have purely genetic causes."},
{"no":6,"text":"In terms of economics, the visit by foreign nationals to witness the XIX Commonwealth Games in India amounted to:","A":"Export","B":"Import","C":"Production","D":"Consumption","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Commonwealth Games","Export","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Foreign visitors spend money on services such as accommodation, transport, food and entertainment within India.\nFrom the perspective of international trade in services, India is providing services to foreign residents.\nTherefore, the expenditure represents an export of services.","revisionPoints":"Export is not limited to physical goods.\nServices supplied to foreign residents can constitute service exports.\nTourism is an important component of trade in services.\nForeign tourists spending money in India bring foreign exchange into the country."},
{"no":7,"text":"Microbial fuel cells are considered a source of sustainable energy. Why?\nThey use living organisms as catalysts to generate electricity from certain substrates.\nThey use a variety of inorganic materials as substrates.\nThey can be installed in wastewater treatment plants to cleanse water and produce electricity.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biotechnology","tags":["Microbial Fuel Cells","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Microbial fuel cells use microorganisms to oxidise organic substrates and generate electrical energy.\nWastewater contains organic matter that can serve as a substrate.\nTherefore, microbial fuel cells can simultaneously contribute to wastewater treatment and electricity generation.\nStatement 2 is incorrect because the relevant substrates are primarily organic materials rather than a variety of inorganic materials.","revisionPoints":"Microbial fuel cell → combines microbiology and electrochemistry.\nMicroorganisms act as biological catalysts.\nCan generate electricity from organic matter.\nPotential application → wastewater treatment."},
{"no":8,"text":"Which one of the following appropriately describes fiscal stimulus?","A":"Massive government investment in manufacturing to meet demand caused by rapid economic growth","B":"Intense affirmative action by the Government to boost economic activity","C":"Government action focused exclusively on loans to agriculture to increase food production","D":"Government action exclusively aimed at financial inclusion","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Fiscal Stimulus","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Fiscal stimulus refers to government measures designed to increase aggregate demand and economic activity.\nIt can include increased government expenditure, tax reductions or other fiscal measures.\nIt is commonly used during periods of weak economic activity.\nThe objective is to stimulate production, employment and demand.","revisionPoints":"Fiscal policy → government taxation and expenditure decisions.\nFiscal stimulus → expansionary fiscal measures.\nGovernment spending can directly increase aggregate demand.\nTax reductions can increase disposable income and consumption."},
{"no":9,"text":"The formation of the ozone hole in the Antarctic region has been a cause of concern. What could be the reason?","A":"Prominent tropospheric turbulence and inflow of chlorofluorocarbons","B":"Prominent polar front and stratospheric clouds, along with inflow of chlorofluorocarbons","C":"Absence of polar front and stratospheric clouds, along with methane and CFCs","D":"Increased temperature in polar regions due to global warming","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Pollution","tags":["Ozone Hole","Antarctic","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Antarctic ozone depletion is strongly associated with chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs) and other ozone-depleting substances.\nDuring the Antarctic winter, polar stratospheric clouds provide surfaces for chemical reactions.\nWhen sunlight returns in spring, these reactions contribute to rapid ozone destruction.\nThe polar atmospheric circulation helps isolate the Antarctic stratosphere during the relevant period.","revisionPoints":"Ozone layer → located mainly in the stratosphere.\nCFCs release chlorine radicals that participate in ozone destruction.\nAntarctic ozone depletion is particularly severe during spring.\nMontreal Protocol is a major international agreement addressing ozone-depleting substances."},
{"no":10,"text":"Consider the following actions:\nDevaluing the domestic currency\nReduction in export subsidy\nPolicies attracting greater FDI and more funds from FIIs\nWhich of the above can help in reducing the current account deficit?","A":"1 and 2","B":"2 and 3","C":"3 only","D":"1 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Current Account Deficit","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Currency depreciation/devaluation can make exports relatively cheaper and imports more expensive, potentially improving the trade balance.\nReducing export subsidies does not directly support export competitiveness in the manner required here.\nGreater FDI and FII inflows help finance external imbalances, although they are capital/financial-account flows rather than current-account receipts.\nHence, according to the source, statements 1 and 3 are correct.","revisionPoints":"Current account includes goods, services, income and transfers.\nCurrency depreciation can influence exports and imports.\nFDI and FII are recorded in the financial account, not the current account.\nExternal financing can help manage a current-account deficit."},
{"no":11,"text":"The Constitution (Seventy-Third Amendment) Act, 1992, relating to Panchayati Raj Institutions, provides for:\nConstitution of District Planning Committees\nState Election Commissions to conduct all Panchayat elections\nEstablishment of State Finance Commissions\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Local Self-Government","tags":["73rd Amendment","Panchayati Raj","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"The 73rd Constitutional Amendment gave constitutional status to Panchayati Raj Institutions.\nIt provides for a State Election Commission for Panchayat elections.\nIt also provides for a State Finance Commission to review the financial position of Panchayats.\nDistrict Planning Committees are associated with Article 243ZD and the 74th Amendment framework concerning municipalities.","revisionPoints":"73rd Amendment → Panchayats.\nAdded Part IX to the Constitution.\nState Election Commission → conducts local-body elections.\nState Finance Commission → reviews finances of Panchayats and Municipalities."},
{"no":12,"text":"Two important rivers—one originating in Jharkhand and known by a different name in Odisha, and another originating in Odisha—merge near the Bay of Bengal before flowing into the sea. The area is important for wildlife and biodiversity and is a protected area. Which one could this be?","A":"Bhitarkanika","B":"Chandipur-on-Sea","C":"Gopalpur-on-Sea","D":"Simlipal","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Bhitarkanika","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Bhitarkanika is an important coastal ecosystem in Odisha.\nIt is characterised by extensive mangrove vegetation, wetlands and estuarine ecosystems.\nThe region is recognised for rich biodiversity and protected-area status.\nIts coastal and riverine setting makes it an important habitat for several species.","revisionPoints":"Bhitarkanika → Odisha.\nFamous for mangrove ecosystems.\nImportant habitat for saltwater crocodiles.\nLocated within a highly productive estuarine/coastal ecosystem."},
{"no":13,"text":"A rapid increase in inflation is sometimes attributed to the base effect. What is the base effect?","A":"Impact of drastic supply deficiency due to crop failure","B":"Impact of a surge in demand due to rapid economic growth","C":"Impact of the previous year's price level on calculation of the inflation rate","D":"None of the above","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Base Effect","Inflation","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Inflation is generally calculated by comparing the current price level with a previous-period base.\nIf the previous year's price level was unusually high or low, the calculated inflation rate can change significantly even without a proportionate change in current prices.\nThis statistical influence is called the base effect.","revisionPoints":"Base effect → influence of the comparison period.\nHigh previous-year base can make current inflation appear lower.\nLow previous-year base can make current inflation appear higher.\nImportant when interpreting year-on-year inflation data."},
{"no":14,"text":"India is regarded as a country with a demographic dividend because of:","A":"High population below 15 years","B":"High population in the 15–64 age group","C":"High population above 65 years","D":"High total population","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Demographic Dividend","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Demographic dividend refers to the potential economic benefit arising when the working-age population forms a relatively large share of the population.\nA larger working-age population can increase labour supply.\nThe benefit is not automatic; it depends on employment, education, healthcare and skill development.\nTherefore, demographic structure creates an opportunity rather than a guaranteed economic outcome.","revisionPoints":"Demographic dividend → opportunity from a favourable age structure.\nWorking-age population → generally 15–64 years in this framework.\nRequires adequate jobs, skills, education and health.\nPopulation ageing can eventually reduce the demographic-dividend window."},
{"no":15,"text":"Regarding carbon credits, which statement is NOT correct?","A":"The carbon credit system was ratified in conjunction with the Kyoto Protocol.","B":"Carbon credits are awarded to countries or groups that reduce greenhouse gases below their emission quota.","C":"The goal of the carbon credit system is to limit increases in carbon dioxide emissions.","D":"Carbon credits are traded at a price fixed from time to time by the United Nations Environment Programme.","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Climate Change","tags":["Carbon Credits","Kyoto Protocol","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Carbon markets use tradable units associated with greenhouse-gas emissions or reductions.\nThe Kyoto Protocol established important market-based mechanisms such as emissions trading and the Clean Development Mechanism.\nCarbon-credit prices are determined by market conditions and the particular carbon market, not by a fixed price set by UNEP.\nTherefore, statement (d) is incorrect.","revisionPoints":"Carbon markets → use tradable emission/reduction units.\nKyoto Protocol → important framework for international carbon-market mechanisms.\nCarbon pricing creates an economic incentive to reduce emissions.\nCarbon-credit prices can vary according to market demand and supply."},
{"no":16,"text":"Which one of the following is NOT a feature of Value Added Tax (VAT)?","A":"It is a multi-point destination-based system of taxation.","B":"It is levied on value addition at each stage of the production-distribution chain.","C":"It is a tax on final consumption and is ultimately borne by the consumer.","D":"It is basically a subject of the Central Government, with State Governments only acting as facilitators.","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Taxation","tags":["Value Added Tax","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"VAT is a multi-stage tax levied on value added at different stages.\nThe burden ultimately falls on the final consumer.\nUnder India's pre-GST system, VAT on intra-State goods was largely a State-level tax.\nTherefore, statement (d) does not describe VAT correctly.","revisionPoints":"VAT → tax on value addition.\nMulti-stage taxation → credit is generally available for tax paid on inputs.\nFinal burden → generally borne by the consumer.\nGST subsequently replaced the earlier fragmented indirect-tax structure in India."},
{"no":17,"text":"A closed economy is an economy in which:","A":"Money supply is fully controlled","B":"Deficit financing takes place","C":"Only exports take place","D":"Neither exports nor imports take place","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Closed Economy","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"A closed economy has no international trade in goods and services.\nTherefore, it does not engage in exports or imports.\nIts economic activity is confined to domestic production, consumption and investment.\nIn practice, completely closed economies are rare.","revisionPoints":"Closed economy → no international trade.\nOpen economy → participates in international trade and financial flows.\nIndia is an open economy.\nEconomic openness can influence investment, exchange rates and trade balances."},
{"no":18,"text":"When the bark of a tree is removed in a circular fashion all around near its base, it gradually dries up and dies because:","A":"Water from the soil cannot rise to the aerial parts","B":"Roots are starved of energy","C":"Tree is infected by soil microbes","D":"Roots do not receive oxygen for respiration","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Tree Girdling","Phloem","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Removing bark around the trunk interrupts the phloem, which transports sugars produced in the leaves toward the roots.\nThe roots therefore receive insufficient organic nutrients.\nOver time, root metabolism and function are severely affected.\nThis process is known as girdling or ring-barking.","revisionPoints":"Xylem → mainly transports water and minerals upward.\nPhloem → transports organic food/sugars.\nGirdling damages the phloem connection between leaves and roots.\nRoots depend on transported carbohydrates for respiration and energy."},
{"no":19,"text":"The New START treaty is:","A":"A bilateral strategic nuclear arms reduction treaty between the USA and the Russian Federation","B":"A multilateral energy-security treaty among East Asia Summit members","C":"A treaty between Russia and the European Union on energy security","D":"A multilateral BRICS treaty for promotion of trade","ans":"A","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Countries in News","tags":["New START Treaty","USA-Russia","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"New START was a major US–Russia strategic arms-control treaty.\nIt focused on reducing and limiting deployed strategic nuclear weapons and delivery systems.\nIt formed part of the broader framework of bilateral nuclear arms control between the two countries.","revisionPoints":"New START → United States + Russian Federation.\nConcerned with strategic nuclear arms.\nPart of the broader history of US–Russia arms-control agreements.\nArms-control treaties seek to place limits on specified categories of weapons."},
{"no":20,"text":"Three of the following criteria contributed to the recognition of Western Ghats–Sri Lanka and Indo-Burma as biodiversity hotspots:\nSpecies richness\nVegetation density\nEndemism\nEthno-botanical importance\nThreat perception\nAdaptation of flora and fauna to warm and humid conditions\nWhich three are correct?","A":"1, 2 and 6","B":"2, 4 and 6","C":"1, 3 and 5","D":"3, 4 and 6","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Biodiversity Hotspots","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Biodiversity hotspots are identified using criteria relating to high biodiversity and significant threats.\nSpecies richness indicates a high diversity of species.\nEndemism indicates species found naturally in a restricted geographic region.\nThreat perception reflects the extent to which the ecosystem is under pressure.\nVegetation density, ethno-botanical importance and adaptation to warm/humid conditions are not the defining hotspot criteria used here.","revisionPoints":"Biodiversity hotspot → area with exceptional species richness/endemism under significant threat.\nEndemism is a key concept.\nHabitat loss is a major threat to hotspots.\nWestern Ghats–Sri Lanka is one of the recognised hotspot regions associated with India."},
{"no":21,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nCarbon dioxide is not the most important greenhouse gas contributing to global warming.\nIncreased concentration of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere does not remain confined to the lower atmosphere because of the absorption of carbon dioxide by phytoplankton in oceans.\nOceans absorb a significant proportion of atmospheric carbon dioxide.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"2 and 3 only","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Climate Change","tags":["Carbon Dioxide","Phytoplankton","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"The source identifies the absorption of atmospheric CO₂ by phytoplankton in oceans as the relevant process.\nPhytoplankton use CO₂ during photosynthesis.\nStatement 1 is not accepted in the source framing.\nStatement 3, as framed in the question, is not taken as the correct answer.","revisionPoints":"Phytoplankton → important component of the marine carbon cycle.\nPhotosynthesis removes CO₂ from the atmosphere.\nOceans act as major carbon sinks.\nCarbon exchange occurs continuously between atmosphere and oceans."},
{"no":22,"text":"What is the significance of marine upwelling?\nIt brings nutrient-rich deep water to the surface.\nIt increases biological productivity of surface waters.\nIt reduces the availability of nutrients to phytoplankton.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1 and 2 only","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Marine Upwelling","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"According to the source, marine upwelling increases surface-water productivity.\nUpwelling brings deeper water containing nutrients toward the surface.\nThese nutrients support phytoplankton growth and therefore enhance marine productivity.\nStatement 3 is incorrect because upwelling increases, rather than reduces, nutrient availability.","revisionPoints":"Upwelling → movement of deep water toward the surface.\nDeep ocean water can be rich in nutrients.\nNutrients support phytoplankton productivity.\nUpwelling regions are often important fishing grounds."},
{"no":23,"text":"Why does a tropical rain forest regenerate slowly after major disturbance?","A":"High temperature prevents seed germination","B":"Propagules have poor viability","C":"Tropical soils contain no nutrients","D":"Heavy rainfall prevents plant growth","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Tropical Rain Forest","Regeneration","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"The source attributes slow regeneration to the poor viability of propagules.\nPropagules include reproductive structures such as seeds or other plant parts capable of producing new plants.\nSuccessful forest regeneration depends heavily on the availability and viability of such reproductive material.","revisionPoints":"Tropical rainforests → high biodiversity and complex ecosystems.\nRegeneration depends on seed/propagule availability and viability.\nDisturbance can alter forest structure and regeneration.\nTropical forests are important carbon and biodiversity reservoirs."},
{"no":24,"text":"The Himalayas have exceptionally rich species diversity mainly because:","A":"They have the world's highest peaks.","B":"They represent a confluence of different biogeographical zones.","C":"They receive snowfall throughout the year.","D":"They have extensive glaciers.","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Himalayan Biodiversity","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"The Himalayas form a meeting zone between different biogeographical regions.\nThis produces considerable variation in climate, altitude, habitats and ecological conditions.\nSuch environmental diversity supports a large variety of species.\nTherefore, biodiversity is not simply a result of the presence of high peaks.","revisionPoints":"Himalayas → major biodiversity hotspot/region.\nHigh altitudinal variation creates multiple ecological zones.\nDifferent climatic conditions support diverse flora and fauna.\nHimalayan ecosystems are important for water security and biodiversity conservation."},
{"no":25,"text":"Which of the following Central Acts have a bearing on biodiversity conservation in India?\nIndian Forest Act, 1927\nCustoms Act, 1962\nMines and Minerals (Development and Regulation) Act, 1957\nBiological Diversity Act, 2002\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:","A":"1 and 4 only","B":"1, 2 and 3 only","C":"2 and 4 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Environmental Law","tags":["Central Acts","Biodiversity Conservation","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"The source identifies the Indian Forest Act, Customs Act and MMDR Act as Central Acts having a bearing on biodiversity.\nThe Indian Forest Act regulates aspects of forest management and protection.\nCustoms provisions can help regulate the movement of biological resources and wildlife-related materials.\nMining activities can significantly affect habitats and ecosystems.","revisionPoints":"Biodiversity conservation involves multiple laws, not only one environmental statute.\nIndian Forest Act → forest regulation.\nMMDR Act → regulation of mineral development.\nCustoms law can support control over cross-border movement of materials/specimens."},
{"no":26,"text":"Karl Marx explained the development of society and class struggle primarily through:","A":"Liberalism","B":"Utilitarianism","C":"Functionalism","D":"Dialectical materialism","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Political Theory","tags":["Karl Marx","Dialectical Materialism","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Marx's analysis focused on material conditions and economic relations.\nDialectical materialism explains social change through contradictions and conflicts arising within material and social relations.\nClass struggle is central to Marxist analysis of historical development.","revisionPoints":"Karl Marx → associated with Marxism.\nHistorical development is linked to material/economic conditions.\nClass struggle is central to Marxist theory.\nCapitalism involves conflict between different economic classes."},
{"no":27,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding the ionosphere:\nThe ionosphere reflects radio waves because of the presence of ozone.\nLong-wavelength radio waves are reflected by the ionosphere because of their wavelength.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Ionosphere","Radio Communication","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"The ionosphere is electrically charged because of ionisation by solar radiation.\nIts ability to affect radio waves is related to free electrons and the frequency of the waves, not the presence of ozone.\nTherefore, statement 1 is incorrect.\nStatement 2 is also incorrect as framed.","revisionPoints":"Ionosphere → region of the upper atmosphere containing ions and free electrons.\nSolar ultraviolet and X-ray radiation contributes to ionisation.\nImportant for radio-wave propagation.\nIonospheric conditions vary with solar activity and time of day."},
{"no":28,"text":"Which of the following statements best describes a difference between FDI and FII?","A":"FII helps bring better management skills and technology, while FDI only brings capital.","B":"FDI brings better management skills and technology, while FII is only investment in securities.","C":"Both FDI and FII are identical forms of foreign investment.","D":"FDI is always short-term while FII is always long-term.","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["FDI","FII","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"The question's source identifies statement (a) as the correct answer.\nFDI refers to investment involving a lasting interest in an enterprise.\nFII refers to investment in financial securities and generally does not imply the same degree of direct managerial control.\nThe source's distinction should be retained for this question.","revisionPoints":"FDI → Foreign Direct Investment.\nFII → Foreign Institutional Investment.\nFDI generally implies a more lasting interest and greater participation.\nPortfolio-type investment is generally more easily reversible."},
{"no":29,"text":"Bt brinjal was developed primarily with the objective of:","A":"Making brinjal resistant to insect pests","B":"Increasing its vitamin content","C":"Making it resistant to drought","D":"Increasing its shelf life","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biotechnology","tags":["Bt Brinjal","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Bt brinjal incorporates a Bt gene that produces a protein toxic to certain insect pests.\nThe technology is intended to reduce damage caused by target insect pests.\nThis can potentially reduce crop losses and dependence on chemical insecticides.","revisionPoints":"Bt → derived from Bacillus thuringiensis.\nBt technology produces insecticidal proteins.\nBt crops are examples of genetically modified crops.\nBt brinjal was developed mainly for resistance to insect pests."},
{"no":30,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding the Aam Aadmi Bima Yojana:\nIt provides insurance coverage to certain economically vulnerable occupational groups.\nIt is available to persons between 30 and 65 years of age.\nIt provides insurance-related social-security support.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Government Schemes","tags":["Aam Admi Bima Yojana","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Aam Aadmi Bima Yojana was designed as a social-security insurance scheme for specified occupational groups.\nIt provided life insurance and related benefits to eligible beneficiaries.\nStatement 2 is incorrect because the age range given in the question is not the applicable range under the source.","revisionPoints":"AABY → social-security-oriented life insurance scheme.\nTargeted economically vulnerable occupational groups.\nIllustrates the role of insurance in social protection.\nGovernment-supported insurance schemes aim to protect vulnerable households from financial shocks."},
{"no":31,"text":"With reference to Brent crude oil, consider the following:\nBrent is an important crude-oil classification used in international markets.\nIt is associated with crude oil from the North Sea.\nBrent crude is completely free from sulphur.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Mining & Minerals","tags":["Brent Crude Oil","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Brent is a major international crude-oil benchmark.\nThe term originates from crude oil production associated with the North Sea.\nBrent is not completely sulphur-free.\nTherefore, statement 3 is incorrect.","revisionPoints":"Brent crude → major global oil benchmark.\nAssociated with North Sea crude.\nOil quality is influenced by characteristics such as API gravity and sulphur content.\nCrude-oil benchmarks are important for international oil pricing."},
{"no":32,"text":"In a nuclear reactor, heavy water is used primarily to:","A":"Slow down neutrons","B":"Absorb neutrons completely","C":"Increase the speed of neutrons","D":"Produce radioactive fuel","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Nuclear Technology","tags":["Heavy Water","Nuclear Reactor","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Heavy water contains deuterium oxide (D₂O).\nDeuterium is effective as a neutron moderator.\nA moderator slows fast neutrons to energies at which they are more effective in sustaining certain nuclear fission reactions.\nHeavy water is therefore important in several reactor designs.","revisionPoints":"Heavy water → D₂O.\nDeuterium → isotope of hydrogen.\nModerator → slows down fast neutrons.\nHeavy-water reactors can use certain fuels efficiently, including natural uranium in relevant reactor designs."},
{"no":33,"text":"Which of the following benefits are available to religious or linguistic minorities in India?\nThey can establish and administer educational institutions of their choice.\nThey can benefit from programmes such as the Prime Minister's 15-Point Programme.\nThe President automatically nominates a representative of every minority community to Parliament.\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 2 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Fundamental Rights","tags":["National Minority Status","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Article 30 protects the right of minorities to establish and administer educational institutions of their choice.\nGovernment programmes can provide targeted support for minority welfare.\nThere is no general constitutional rule that the President automatically nominates a representative of every minority community to Parliament.","revisionPoints":"Article 29 → protection of interests of minorities and cultural identity.\nArticle 30 → right of minorities to establish and administer educational institutions.\nMinority welfare programmes may address education, employment and socioeconomic development.\nConstitutional protection does not mean automatic nomination to Parliament."},
{"no":34,"text":"Which of the following are benefits provided to persons with disabilities?\nFree education up to 18 years in government schools.\nPreferential allotment of land for certain purposes.\nProvision of ramps in public buildings.\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Fundamental Rights","tags":["Persons with Disabilities","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"The source identifies all three as measures supporting persons with disabilities.\nEducational support aims to improve access to schooling.\nPreferential land allotment can facilitate rehabilitation and livelihood-related opportunities.\nRamps and accessibility infrastructure improve access to public buildings.","revisionPoints":"Disability policy includes education, accessibility and livelihood support.\nAccessibility → physical infrastructure should accommodate persons with disabilities.\nInclusive education seeks to reduce barriers to schooling.\nWelfare measures aim at social and economic inclusion."},
{"no":35,"text":"The primary purpose of Mega Food Parks is to:","A":"Promote organic farming exclusively","B":"Provide infrastructure for food processing and reduce wastage of perishables","C":"Promote only export-oriented food industries","D":"Introduce emerging and eco-friendly technologies exclusively","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Government Schemes","tags":["Mega Food Parks","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Mega Food Parks seek to create modern infrastructure for food processing.\nThey connect farmers, processors, markets and related facilities.\nBetter processing and storage infrastructure can reduce post-harvest losses.\nThe objective is not limited to exports or a particular farming method.","revisionPoints":"Food processing → adds value to agricultural produce.\nMega Food Parks → integrated food-processing infrastructure.\nCan reduce post-harvest losses.\nBetter cold-chain and processing facilities improve supply-chain efficiency."},
{"no":36,"text":"Withdrawal of money from the Consolidated Fund of India requires:","A":"Approval of the President alone","B":"Authorisation by Parliament","C":"Approval of the Finance Commission","D":"Approval of the Comptroller and Auditor General","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Parliament","tags":["Consolidated Fund of India","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"The Consolidated Fund contains major government revenues and receipts.\nMoney cannot ordinarily be withdrawn from it without parliamentary authorisation.\nThis reflects the constitutional principle of legislative control over public finances.\nThe CAG audits expenditure but does not authorise withdrawals.","revisionPoints":"Consolidated Fund of India → major account of Union government finances.\nWithdrawal → requires Parliamentary authorisation.\nAppropriation Act provides authority for expenditure.\nFinancial control is an important function of Parliament."},
{"no":37,"text":"Which of the following receipts of the Union Government are credited to the Consolidated Fund of India?\nUnion taxes\nOther revenues of the Union Government\nPublic account deposits only\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 2 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Public Finance","tags":["Consolidated Fund of India","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Union tax revenues form an important component of the Consolidated Fund.\nOther revenues received by the Union Government are also credited to it.\nPublic Account is constitutionally distinct from the Consolidated Fund.\nTherefore, statement 3 is incorrect.","revisionPoints":"Consolidated Fund → major government receipts and expenditure.\nPublic Account → money held by government in a trustee-like capacity.\nContingency Fund → used for urgent unforeseen expenditure.\nThese are distinct constitutional financial accounts."},
{"no":38,"text":"Which of the following are generally included among microfinance services?\nCredit\nSavings\nInsurance\nFund transfer/remittance services\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1, 2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking & Monetary Policy","tags":["Microfinance","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Microfinance is broader than merely providing small loans.\nIt can include credit, savings, insurance and money-transfer services.\nSuch services are intended to improve financial access among underserved populations.\nMicrofinance can therefore contribute to financial inclusion.","revisionPoints":"Microfinance → financial services for low-income/underserved groups.\nIncludes microcredit and may include savings and insurance.\nSupports financial inclusion.\nSelf-help groups and microfinance institutions can play important roles in rural finance."},
{"no":39,"text":"Southeast Asia has acquired special geostrategic importance because:\nIt lies between the Pacific and Indian Oceans.\nIt has extensive maritime connections.\nIt has no importance for global trade routes.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1 and 2 only","ans":"D","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Regional Organizations","tags":["Southeast Asia","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Southeast Asia occupies a strategically important position between the Indian and Pacific Oceans.\nMajor maritime routes pass through the region.\nChokepoints and straits in the region are important for international commerce.\nStatement 3 is incorrect because the region has major importance for global trade.","revisionPoints":"Southeast Asia → Indo-Pacific strategic region.\nImportant maritime routes connect the Indian and Pacific Oceans.\nStrait of Malacca is a major global maritime chokepoint.\nRegional geography has major implications for trade and maritime security."},
{"no":40,"text":"A food product carrying the label “No Trans-fats” indicates that:\nIt is not made using hydrogenated oils.\nThe oils used are unlikely to damage cardiovascular health.\nIt does not contain any animal fats or oils.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 2 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Trans-fats","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Industrial trans fats are commonly associated with partial hydrogenation of vegetable oils.\nEliminating industrial trans fats is beneficial from a cardiovascular-health perspective.\n“No trans-fats” does not necessarily mean that a product contains no animal fats.\nTherefore, statement 3 is incorrect.","revisionPoints":"Trans fats → associated with increased cardiovascular disease risk.\nIndustrial trans fats are commonly produced through partial hydrogenation.\nReducing trans-fat intake is a public-health objective.\n“No trans-fats” does not mean “fat-free.”"},
{"no":41,"text":"Consider the following statement regarding the Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Act (MGNREGA):\nWho among the following are eligible to demand employment under MGNREGA?","A":"Only persons below the poverty line","B":"Only landless agricultural labourers","C":"Only rural women","D":"Adult members of any rural household willing to do unskilled manual work","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Government Schemes","tags":["MGNREGA","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"MGNREGA provides a legal guarantee of employment to eligible rural households.\nAny adult member of such a household can demand employment.\nThe work is generally unskilled manual work.\nEligibility is not restricted only to BPL households, landless labourers or women.","revisionPoints":"MGNREGA → rights-based employment programme.\nProvides up to 100 days of guaranteed wage employment per rural household in a financial year, subject to the Act.\nEmployment is demand-driven.\nFocuses on livelihood security and creation of rural assets."},
{"no":42,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding India's Look East Policy:\nIt was initiated to strengthen economic and strategic relations with Southeast Asian countries.\nIt was primarily intended to establish military alliances with Southeast Asian countries.\nIt helped strengthen India's engagement with ASEAN.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 3 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Regional Organizations","tags":["Look East Policy","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"India's Look East Policy aimed to increase India's economic, political and strategic engagement with East and Southeast Asia.\nASEAN became an important partner in this engagement.\nThe policy was not primarily about establishing military alliances.\nIt subsequently evolved into the Act East Policy.","revisionPoints":"Look East Policy → initiated in the early 1990s.\nFocus → stronger engagement with East and Southeast Asia.\nASEAN → important pillar of India's eastern engagement.\nAct East Policy represents a more active and strategic evolution of the approach."},
{"no":43,"text":"If the Annual Financial Statement/Budget is not passed by the Lok Sabha, what happens?","A":"The President dissolves the Lok Sabha immediately.","B":"The Finance Minister automatically becomes Prime Minister.","C":"The Supreme Court takes over the financial administration.","D":"The Council of Ministers is expected to resign.","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Parliament","tags":["Union Budget","No-Confidence","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"The Council of Ministers is collectively responsible to the Lok Sabha.\nFailure to secure the Lok Sabha's confidence on a matter as fundamental as the budget can amount to loss of confidence.\nIn the source's constitutional framing, the Council of Ministers would therefore be expected to resign.","revisionPoints":"Council of Ministers → collectively responsible to Lok Sabha.\nMoney cannot ordinarily be withdrawn from the Consolidated Fund without parliamentary authorisation.\nBudgetary approval reflects legislative control over public finance.\nLoss of Lok Sabha confidence can require the Council of Ministers to resign."},
{"no":44,"text":"Which one of the following is NOT a Fundamental Duty under the Constitution of India?","A":"To vote in public elections","B":"To develop scientific temper","C":"To protect the environment","D":"To strive towards excellence in all spheres of individual and collective activity","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["Fundamental Duties","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Voting is an important democratic activity, but it is not listed as a Fundamental Duty under Article 51A.\nDeveloping scientific temper is a Fundamental Duty.\nProtecting the natural environment is a Fundamental Duty.\nCitizens are also expected to strive for excellence.","revisionPoints":"Fundamental Duties → Article 51A.\nAdded by the 42nd Constitutional Amendment, 1976.\nOriginally there were 10 Fundamental Duties.\nThe 11th duty concerning education of children was added by the 86th Amendment, 2002."},
{"no":45,"text":"Consider the following statements about the Finance Commission:\nIt is constituted by the President.\nIt is constituted every five years or earlier if necessary.\nIt recommends the distribution of certain tax revenues between the Union and the States.\nWhich of the above statements are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Public Finance","tags":["Finance Commission","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"The Finance Commission is a constitutional body under Article 280.\nIt is constituted by the President.\nIt is normally constituted every five years, or earlier if considered necessary.\nOne of its key functions is recommending the distribution of tax revenues between the Union and States.","revisionPoints":"Finance Commission → Article 280.\nConstituted by the President.\nNormally constituted every five years.\nDeals with fiscal federalism and Centre-State financial relations."},
{"no":46,"text":"Which of the following are recognised as human rights under the Universal Declaration of Human Rights (UDHR)?\nRight to education\nEqual access to public service\nRight to food\nRight to unlimited private property\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1, 2 and 4 only","D":"1, 2 and 3 only","ans":"D","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Organizations","tags":["Universal Declaration of Human Rights","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"The UDHR recognises a broad range of civil, political, economic, social and cultural rights.\nIt recognises the right to education.\nIt also recognises equal access to public service.\nThe right to an adequate standard of living includes access to food.\nIt does not establish an unlimited right to private property.","revisionPoints":"UDHR adopted by the UN General Assembly in 1948.\nContains both civil-political and socio-economic rights.\nEducation is recognised as a human right.\nHuman rights are intended to apply universally, irrespective of nationality or status."},
{"no":47,"text":"Which of the following factors can contribute to harmful algal blooms?\nNutrient-rich water from estuaries\nMonsoon runoff carrying nutrients\nOcean upwelling bringing nutrients to surface waters\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Pollution","tags":["Harmful Algal Blooms","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Algal blooms can occur when waters receive excessive nutrients, particularly nitrogen and phosphorus.\nEstuarine inputs can carry nutrients into coastal waters.\nMonsoon runoff can transport agricultural and other nutrients.\nUpwelling can bring nutrient-rich deep water to the surface and stimulate biological productivity.","revisionPoints":"Harmful algal blooms → excessive proliferation of certain algae/cyanobacteria.\nNutrient enrichment → eutrophication.\nRunoff from agriculture can contribute nutrients.\nSome harmful blooms can produce toxins and cause fish kills."},
{"no":48,"text":"Which of the following processes add carbon dioxide to the atmosphere?\nRespiration\nDecay of organic matter\nVolcanic activity\nPhotosynthesis\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:","A":"1 and 4 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1, 2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Carbon Cycle","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Respiration releases CO₂ into the atmosphere.\nDecomposition/decay of organic matter releases carbon compounds, including CO₂.\nVolcanic activity can release CO₂ stored within the Earth's interior.\nPhotosynthesis does the opposite: it removes CO₂ from the atmosphere.","revisionPoints":"Respiration → releases CO₂.\nDecomposition → returns carbon to the atmosphere.\nVolcanism → natural geological source of CO₂.\nPhotosynthesis → major biological carbon sink."},
{"no":49,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nThe Australia Group is a military alliance of countries in the Indo-Pacific region.\nThe Wassenaar Arrangement is a treaty for reducing conventional military forces.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"D","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Organizations","tags":["Australia Group","Wassenaar Arrangement","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"The Australia Group is an informal multilateral export-control arrangement focused on preventing the proliferation of chemical and biological weapons.\nIt is not a military alliance.\nThe Wassenaar Arrangement concerns export controls relating to conventional arms and dual-use goods and technologies.\nIt is not a treaty for reducing conventional military forces.","revisionPoints":"Australia Group → controls related to chemical and biological weapons proliferation.\nWassenaar Arrangement → export controls on conventional arms and dual-use technologies.\nBoth are associated with non-proliferation/export control.\nThey are not conventional military alliances."},
{"no":50,"text":"Why does the bottom of a lake remain liquid even when the surface is covered with ice during winter?","A":"Ice is a poor conductor of heat.","B":"Water expands continuously as it cools below 4°C.","C":"Water has maximum density at about 4°C.","D":"The lake bottom receives direct solar radiation.","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Density of Water","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Water behaves unusually compared with most substances.\nIts density is maximum at approximately 4°C.\nAs surface water cools to 4°C, it becomes denser and sinks.\nFurther cooling below 4°C makes water less dense, so it remains near the surface and eventually freezes.\nThe ice layer acts as an insulating cover, allowing liquid water to remain below.","revisionPoints":"Maximum density of freshwater → approximately 4°C.\nIce is less dense than liquid water, so it floats.\nThis unusual property supports aquatic life in cold climates.\nIce acts as a thermal insulator."},
{"no":51,"text":"An animal found mainly in a sandy saline region with very little vegetation and few natural predators is most likely:","A":"Nilgai","B":"Indian Wild Ass","C":"One-horned Rhinoceros","D":"Sangai","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Indian Wild Ass","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"The Indian Wild Ass is adapted to the arid and semi-arid landscape of the Rann of Kachchh.\nThe habitat contains extensive saline and sandy terrain.\nSuch conditions are unsuitable for many large herbivores.\nThe species is therefore strongly associated with this distinctive landscape.","revisionPoints":"Indian Wild Ass → Rann of Kachchh, Gujarat.\nScientific name → Equus hemionus khur.\nAdapted to arid and saline environments.\nWild Ass Sanctuary is an important conservation area for the species."},
{"no":52,"text":"Consider the following statements about La Niña and El Niño:\nLa Niña is associated with unusually warm sea-surface temperatures in the central and eastern tropical Pacific.\nEl Niño is associated with unusually cold sea-surface temperatures in the same region.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["La Nina","El Nino","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"The statements have the characteristics of El Niño and La Niña reversed.\nEl Niño → unusually warm sea-surface temperatures in the central/eastern tropical Pacific.\nLa Niña → unusually cool sea-surface temperatures in the same region.\nBoth are phases of the ENSO phenomenon.","revisionPoints":"ENSO → El Niño–Southern Oscillation.\nEl Niño → warming of central/eastern tropical Pacific.\nLa Niña → cooling of the same region.\nENSO can influence Indian monsoon variability and global weather patterns."},
{"no":53,"text":"Why did litigation increase significantly after the Permanent Settlement introduced under Cornwallis?","A":"Zamindars were given complete judicial powers.","B":"Ryots were given extensive rights over zamindars.","C":"The British introduced a uniform judicial system throughout India.","D":"None of the above","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Lord Cornwallis","Permanent Settlement","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"The source does not identify options (a), (b) or (c) as the correct explanation.\nThe Permanent Settlement created new revenue and property relationships involving zamindars and the colonial state.\nChanges in property rights and revenue obligations generated disputes and litigation.\nThe source's answer is therefore (d).","revisionPoints":"Permanent Settlement → introduced by Lord Cornwallis in 1793.\nMainly associated with Bengal, Bihar and parts of Odisha.\nZamindars were recognised as revenue-collecting landholders.\nLand revenue demand was fixed permanently under the settlement."},
{"no":54,"text":"Which of the following observations about the Quit India Movement is NOT correct?","A":"It was launched in August 1942.","B":"It witnessed widespread participation in many parts of India.","C":"Parallel governments emerged in some areas.","D":"It did not attract the labour class in general.","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Quit India Movement","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"The Quit India Movement began in August 1942 after the Bombay session of the Indian National Congress.\nIt developed into a widespread mass movement.\nParallel governments were established in some places.\nWorkers and labour groups also participated in different regions, making statement (d) incorrect.","revisionPoints":"Quit India Movement → launched in August 1942.\nSlogan associated with Gandhi → “Do or Die.”\nMajor leaders were arrested soon after the movement began.\nUnderground networks and parallel administrations emerged in several areas."},
{"no":55,"text":"A common factor behind several tribal uprisings during British rule was:","A":"Introduction of modern education","B":"Introduction of railways","C":"Rise of moneylenders, traders and revenue farmers as intermediaries","D":"Expansion of irrigation facilities","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Tribal Insurrection","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Colonial revenue arrangements often brought tribal communities into greater contact with moneylenders, traders and revenue intermediaries.\nThese groups could acquire economic influence over tribal land and resources.\nDebt, land alienation and exploitation contributed to several tribal uprisings.\nThese factors combined with changes in traditional tribal economic systems.","revisionPoints":"Tribal uprisings → often linked to land alienation and exploitation.\nMoneylenders and traders could become powerful intermediaries.\nColonial forest and revenue policies affected traditional livelihoods.\nImportant examples include Santhal, Munda and Kol uprisings."},
{"no":56,"text":"The maritime links between India and the countries of the Bay of Bengal were facilitated historically by:","A":"Permanent trade winds","B":"Ocean currents alone","C":"Monsoon winds","D":"Polar winds","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Maritime Trade","Bay of Bengal","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"The seasonal reversal of monsoon winds made maritime travel across the Indian Ocean and Bay of Bengal more predictable.\nAncient and medieval sailors used these seasonal wind patterns to plan voyages.\nThis facilitated trade and cultural interaction between India and Southeast Asia.","revisionPoints":"Indian Ocean trade → strongly influenced by seasonal monsoon winds.\nMonsoon reversal enabled relatively predictable outward and return voyages.\nMaritime trade facilitated the spread of religion, culture, language and technology.\nBay of Bengal served as an important historical maritime corridor."},
{"no":57,"text":"With reference to Bluetooth and Wi-Fi, consider the following statements:\nBluetooth generally operates in the 2.4 GHz frequency band.\nWi-Fi can operate in both 2.4 GHz and 5 GHz bands.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"Both 1 and 2","B":"1 only","C":"2 only","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"IT & Computers","tags":["Bluetooth","Wi-Fi","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Bluetooth commonly operates in the 2.4 GHz ISM band.\nWi-Fi can operate in the 2.4 GHz band and, depending on the standard, in the 5 GHz band.\nThese technologies use radio waves for wireless communication.\nTheir differences also include range, power consumption and typical applications.","revisionPoints":"Bluetooth → short-range wireless communication.\nBluetooth → commonly uses 2.4 GHz.\nWi-Fi → can use 2.4 GHz and 5 GHz bands.\nWi-Fi generally provides higher data rates and network connectivity than Bluetooth."},
{"no":58,"text":"Consider the following statements about micro-irrigation:\nIt reduces loss of water and nutrients from agricultural fields.\nIt can help check the depletion of groundwater in some regions.\nIt is the only possible irrigation method for dryland agriculture.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 2 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Micro-irrigation","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Drip and sprinkler systems can deliver water more efficiently than conventional surface irrigation.\nEfficient application can reduce water loss and nutrient leaching.\nReduced water demand can help mitigate groundwater depletion in suitable areas.\nMicro-irrigation is not the only irrigation method available for dryland agriculture.","revisionPoints":"Micro-irrigation → mainly drip and sprinkler systems.\nImproves water-use efficiency.\nCan support fertigation, combining irrigation and nutrient application.\nParticularly useful where water resources are limited."},
{"no":59,"text":"Which of the following were included under the colonial-era Home Charges?\nExpenses of the India Office in London\nSalaries and pensions of certain British personnel serving in India\nExpenses of wars outside India borne by India\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Home Charges","Drain of Wealth","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Home Charges represented expenses incurred in Britain but charged to Indian revenues.\nThey included expenses of the India Office in London.\nSalaries and pensions of certain British officials were also included.\nSome military and diplomatic expenses incurred outside India were charged to Indian revenues.","revisionPoints":"Home Charges → important component of the economic drain from India.\nPaid from Indian revenues for expenses incurred in Britain.\nIncluded administrative and pension-related expenses.\nDrain theory was prominently articulated by Dadabhai Naoroji."},
{"no":60,"text":"The Kheda Satyagraha of 1918 was launched mainly because:\nThe government refused to suspend or reduce land revenue collection despite crop failure.\nThe movement was directed against the Rowlatt Act.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Kheda Satyagraha","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Kheda in Gujarat faced crop failure and economic distress.\nPeasants demanded suspension/reduction of land revenue because they were unable to meet the assessment.\nGandhi, Sardar Patel and other leaders supported the movement.\nThe Rowlatt Act came later and concerned a different issue.","revisionPoints":"Kheda Satyagraha → 1918, Gujarat.\nMain issue → land revenue during crop failure.\nAssociated with Mahatma Gandhi and Sardar Patel.\nIt was an important early example of Gandhian mass mobilisation in India."},
{"no":61,"text":"Which of the following are ecosystem services provided by biodiversity?\nSoil formation\nPrevention of soil erosion\nRecycling of waste\nPollination of crops\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1, 3 and 4 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Ecosystem Services","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Biodiversity supports several ecosystem processes and services.\nOrganisms contribute to soil formation and nutrient cycling.\nVegetation and biological communities help prevent soil erosion.\nDecomposers contribute to recycling organic matter.\nPollinators are essential for the reproduction of many crops.","revisionPoints":"Ecosystem services → benefits humans obtain from ecosystems.\nProvisioning services → food, water, timber.\nRegulating services → climate regulation, pollination, erosion control.\nSupporting services → soil formation and nutrient cycling."},
{"no":62,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding aspartame:\nIt is several times sweeter than ordinary sugar.\nSmall quantities can provide sweetness with fewer calories than equivalent quantities of sugar.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Aspartame","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Aspartame is an artificial low-calorie sweetener.\nIt is considerably sweeter than sucrose.\nBecause only a small amount is needed, it contributes relatively few calories when used in foods and beverages.\nIt is therefore used as a sugar substitute.","revisionPoints":"Aspartame → high-intensity artificial sweetener.\nMuch sweeter than sucrose.\nUsed in some low-calorie food and beverage products.\nHigh-intensity sweeteners require much smaller quantities than sugar."},
{"no":63,"text":"The Indian Parliamentary Committee formed in 1893 under the leadership of William Wedderburn and others was mainly concerned with:","A":"Agitating for Indian political reforms in the British House of Commons","B":"Demanding immediate independence from British rule","C":"Establishing provincial governments in India","D":"Promoting industrialisation in India","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Indian Parliamentary Committee 1893","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"The committee was formed to create support for Indian political and constitutional reforms in Britain.\nIt sought to influence opinion within the British Parliament.\nThis reflected the early nationalist strategy of using constitutional and political pressure in Britain.","revisionPoints":"William Wedderburn → associated with early Indian nationalist political activity in Britain.\nEarly nationalists used constitutional methods and public opinion.\nBritish Parliament was an important arena for raising Indian political demands.\nSuch efforts preceded the later phase of more mass-based nationalism."},
{"no":64,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding CFL and LED lamps:\nCFLs use mercury vapour and phosphor to produce light.\nLEDs use semiconductor materials.\nCFLs are generally less energy-efficient than LEDs.\nCFLs have a longer average life than LEDs.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1, 2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"IT & Computers","tags":["CFL","LED","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"CFLs generate light using mercury vapour and phosphor coating.\nLEDs produce light through semiconductor-based technology.\nLEDs generally consume less electricity for comparable illumination.\nLEDs also generally have a longer operating life, making statement 4 incorrect.","revisionPoints":"CFL → Compact Fluorescent Lamp.\nCFLs contain a small amount of mercury.\nLED → Light Emitting Diode.\nLEDs generally offer high energy efficiency and long service life."},
{"no":65,"text":"What is Oilzapper?","A":"An eco-friendly technology for treating oily sludge and oil spills","B":"A machine used to increase petroleum extraction","C":"A device used to detect underground oil reserves","D":"A chemical used to increase the octane number of petrol","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Environmental Technology","tags":["Oilzapper","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Oilzapper is based on bioremediation.\nMicroorganisms are used to degrade hydrocarbons present in oily waste.\nIt can be applied to contaminated soil, oily sludge and areas affected by oil spills.\nIt provides an environmentally oriented approach to petroleum-contamination management.","revisionPoints":"Oilzapper → bioremediation technology.\nUses microorganisms to degrade petroleum hydrocarbons.\nApplication → oily sludge and contaminated sites.\nBiotechnology can provide environmentally friendly pollution-control solutions."},
{"no":66,"text":"A couple has blood groups AB+ and O−. Their twins have blood groups A+ and B+. What can be concluded about the blood group of their adopted son?","A":"He must be O+","B":"He must be AB+","C":"He must be A+ or B+","D":"His blood group cannot be determined from the information given","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Blood Groups","Genetics","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"The adopted son is not genetically related to the couple.\nTherefore, the blood groups of the biological parents and twins do not determine his blood group.\nBlood-group inheritance can only be inferred when the relevant biological parental relationship is known.\nHence, his blood group cannot be determined from the given information.","revisionPoints":"ABO blood group → determined genetically.\nRh factor → commonly represented as positive or negative.\nBiological parentage is essential for inheritance-based blood-group deductions.\nAdoption does not create a genetic relationship."},
{"no":67,"text":"Which statement best describes the impact of John Ruskin's Unto This Last on Mahatma Gandhi?","A":"It convinced Gandhi to adopt complete political isolation.","B":"It gave Gandhi the idea that the good of the individual is contained in the good of all.","C":"It persuaded Gandhi to reject village industries.","D":"It led Gandhi to support unrestricted industrialisation.","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Unto This Last","Mahatma Gandhi","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Gandhi was deeply influenced by Ruskin's ideas on social welfare and the dignity of labour.\nThe work reinforced the idea that individual welfare cannot be separated from the welfare of society.\nGandhi later translated these ideas into his concepts of Sarvodaya and constructive work.","revisionPoints":"Unto This Last → written by John Ruskin.\nGandhi was strongly influenced by the work.\nEmphasised welfare of all and dignity of labour.\nGandhi's interpretation contributed to the idea of Sarvodaya."},
{"no":68,"text":"Who among the following is associated with the operation of the secret Congress Radio during the Quit India Movement?","A":"Usha Mehta","B":"Sarojini Naidu","C":"Aruna Asaf Ali","D":"Sucheta Kriplani","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Usha Mehta","Congress Radio","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Usha Mehta helped operate the underground Congress Radio during the Quit India Movement.\nThe radio provided information and nationalist messages when the colonial government had suppressed much of the regular nationalist press.\nIt became an important example of underground communication during the movement.","revisionPoints":"Usha Mehta → associated with Congress Radio, 1942.\nQuit India Movement → launched in August 1942.\nUnderground communication helped bypass colonial censorship.\nCongress Radio operated for a limited period during the movement."},
{"no":69,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding Blu-ray discs and DVDs:\nDVDs generally store standard-definition video, while Blu-ray can store high-definition video.\nBlu-ray discs can store several times more data than conventional DVDs.\nBlu-ray discs are considerably thicker than DVDs.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"IT & Computers","tags":["Blu-ray","DVD","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Blu-ray technology uses a blue-violet laser, allowing data to be stored at a higher density.\nThis gives Blu-ray discs substantially greater storage capacity than DVDs.\nBlu-ray technology is commonly associated with high-definition video.\nThe physical thickness statement is incorrect.","revisionPoints":"DVD → uses a red laser.\nBlu-ray → uses a shorter-wavelength blue-violet laser.\nShorter wavelength allows greater data-storage density.\nBlu-ray → higher storage capacity than standard DVD."},
{"no":70,"text":"Which of the following were recommendations of the Nehru Report (1928)?\nJoint electorates with reservation of seats for minorities in certain circumstances.\nFundamental rights.\nComplete independence from British rule as the immediate constitutional objective.\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Nehru Report","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"The Nehru Report proposed a constitutional framework for self-government in India.\nIt recommended joint electorates, with reservations for minorities in specified circumstances.\nIt also proposed a list of fundamental rights.\nComplete independence was not its immediate constitutional objective.","revisionPoints":"Nehru Report → 1928.\nChairman → Motilal Nehru.\nProposed constitutional reforms and Dominion Status.\nIncluded provisions relating to fundamental rights and representation."},
{"no":71,"text":"Which Indian State has particularly favourable conditions for orchid cultivation and significant export potential?","A":"Rajasthan","B":"Arunachal Pradesh","C":"Punjab","D":"Gujarat","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Orchid Cultivation","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Arunachal Pradesh has a wide range of altitudes, climates and forest ecosystems.\nThese varied ecological conditions support a rich diversity of orchids.\nThe State has significant potential for orchid cultivation and associated horticultural activities.","revisionPoints":"Arunachal Pradesh → part of the Eastern Himalayas.\nKnown for exceptionally rich floral biodiversity.\nOrchids are particularly diverse in humid Himalayan environments.\nHorticulture can contribute to livelihood diversification in the Northeast."},
{"no":72,"text":"Which one of the following is NOT an example of in-situ conservation?","A":"National Park","B":"Botanical Garden","C":"Wildlife Sanctuary","D":"Biosphere Reserve","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["In-situ Conservation","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"In-situ conservation protects organisms in their natural habitats.\nNational Parks, Wildlife Sanctuaries and Biosphere Reserves are examples of in-situ conservation.\nA botanical garden maintains plants outside their natural habitats, making it an ex-situ method.","revisionPoints":"In-situ → conservation within natural habitat.\nExamples → National Parks, Wildlife Sanctuaries, Biosphere Reserves.\nEx-situ → conservation outside natural habitat.\nExamples → botanical gardens, zoos, seed banks and gene banks."},
{"no":73,"text":"Consider the following statements about the Metropolitan Planning Committee:\nIt is a constitutional body.\nIt prepares a draft development plan for the metropolitan area.\nIt is solely responsible for implementing all development projects in the metropolitan area.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Local Self-Government","tags":["Metropolitan Planning Committee","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Metropolitan Planning Committees are provided for under the 74th Constitutional Amendment.\nThey prepare a draft development plan for the metropolitan area.\nTheir role is primarily planning and coordination, not sole implementation of every development project.\nTherefore, statement 3 is incorrect.","revisionPoints":"Metropolitan Planning Committee → Article 243ZE.\nAssociated with the 74th Constitutional Amendment.\nIntended for integrated planning of metropolitan areas.\nDistrict Planning Committee → Article 243ZD."},
{"no":74,"text":"What is the difference between a Vote-on-Account and an Interim Budget?","A":"Vote-on-Account deals with taxation, while Interim Budget deals only with expenditure.","B":"Vote-on-Account is passed by the Rajya Sabha, while Interim Budget is passed by the Lok Sabha.","C":"Vote-on-Account mainly provides authority for government expenditure for a limited period, while an Interim Budget also presents estimates of receipts and expenditure.","D":"There is no difference between them.","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Public Finance","tags":["Vote-on-Account","Interim Budget","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"A Vote-on-Account provides temporary parliamentary authorisation for government expenditure.\nIt is generally used when the full budget has not yet been passed.\nAn Interim Budget presents a broader statement of the government's finances, including receipts and expenditure.\nTherefore, they are related but not identical.","revisionPoints":"Vote-on-Account → temporary expenditure authorisation.\nInterim Budget → broader financial statement.\nBoth may be used during a transition period.\nParliamentary control remains central to government expenditure."},
{"no":75,"text":"The International Monetary Fund (IMF) provides loans primarily to:","A":"Any country affected by natural disasters","B":"Only developed countries","C":"Member countries","D":"Only countries belonging to the G20","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"International Financial Institutions","tags":["IMF","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"The IMF is an international financial institution whose members are sovereign countries.\nIts financial assistance is available to member countries facing balance-of-payments and related external financial difficulties.\nIMF programmes are generally associated with macroeconomic and financial stability.","revisionPoints":"IMF → established at Bretton Woods.\nMain functions → monetary cooperation, financial assistance and macroeconomic surveillance.\nIMF assistance is available to member countries.\nWorld Bank and IMF have different primary institutional roles."},
{"no":76,"text":"Mangrove forests act as a natural safety hedge against storms and tides mainly because:","A":"They prevent all coastal flooding.","B":"They increase the salinity of coastal waters.","C":"They completely stop tidal movement.","D":"Their extensive root systems help resist erosion and reduce the impact of waves and storms.","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Mangroves","Tsunami","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Mangroves have complex and extensive root systems.\nThese roots trap sediments and stabilise coastal soils.\nThey can reduce wave energy and help protect shorelines from erosion.\nMangroves therefore provide important coastal protection ecosystem services.","revisionPoints":"Mangroves → salt-tolerant coastal ecosystems.\nExtensive roots → sediment trapping and shoreline stabilisation.\nProvide habitat for fish, birds and other organisms.\nAct as natural buffers against storms and coastal erosion."},
{"no":77,"text":"According to Jain philosophy, the universe is:","A":"Created and maintained by a Universal Law","B":"Created by a single supreme creator","C":"Created by the Vedic gods","D":"Continuously created and destroyed by human actions","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Jain Philosophy","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Jain philosophy does not posit a creator God responsible for creating the universe.\nThe universe is understood through eternal principles and laws.\nReality consists of fundamental categories such as jiva and ajiva.\nThe universe undergoes cycles rather than being created by a supreme creator.","revisionPoints":"Jainism → non-theistic in the creator-God sense.\nJiva → living/conscious substance.\nAjiva → non-living categories.\nJain philosophy emphasises karma, liberation and non-violence."},
{"no":78,"text":"Which one of the following is an important effect of soil salinization?","A":"It makes all soils highly fertile.","B":"It can make some soils impermeable and reduce agricultural productivity.","C":"It increases groundwater recharge everywhere.","D":"It eliminates the need for irrigation.","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Pollution","tags":["Soil Salinization","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Salinization occurs when soluble salts accumulate in soil.\nExcess salts can adversely affect plant water uptake.\nSevere salinity can alter soil structure and reduce permeability.\nThis can lower agricultural productivity and make cultivation difficult.","revisionPoints":"Soil salinization → accumulation of soluble salts.\nCommon in areas with inadequate drainage and excessive irrigation.\nCan cause soil degradation and crop-yield reduction.\nProper drainage and water management help control salinity."},
{"no":79,"text":"The IUCN Red Data Books contain information primarily about:","A":"Only extinct species","B":"Threatened plant and animal species","C":"Only endangered forests","D":"Only marine organisms","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"International Environmental Bodies","tags":["Red Data Books","IUCN","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"The IUCN Red List system assesses the conservation status of species.\nIt includes categories ranging from Least Concern to Extinct, with several threatened categories.\nThe system covers both plants and animals.\nIt is an important reference for biodiversity conservation.","revisionPoints":"IUCN → International Union for Conservation of Nature.\nRed List categories include Critically Endangered, Endangered and Vulnerable.\n“Threatened” generally refers to the three categories above.\nConservation status helps guide conservation priorities and policy."},
{"no":80,"text":"Why are teaser loans considered a concern for banks?\nThey may involve initially low interest rates followed by higher rates later.\nThey can expose banks to future repayment/default risks.\nThey are necessarily given only to inexperienced entrepreneurs.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking & Monetary Policy","tags":["Teaser Loans","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Teaser loans may offer attractive initial interest rates.\nBorrowers may face higher repayment obligations when the introductory period ends.\nThis can increase the risk of repayment difficulties and future defaults.\nThe loans are not necessarily restricted to inexperienced entrepreneurs.","revisionPoints":"Teaser loan → typically has initially attractive interest terms.\nLater rate increases can raise the borrower's repayment burden.\nBanks face credit/default risk.\nBorrowers should assess the full loan structure, not only the introductory rate."},
{"no":81,"text":"Why does a satellite orbiting the Earth not simply fall onto the Earth?","A":"There is no gravitational force acting on the satellite.","B":"The satellite is outside the Earth's gravitational field.","C":"The satellite continuously moves away from the Earth.","D":"Earth's gravitational attraction provides the necessary acceleration for its orbital motion.","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Space","tags":["Satellite Orbit","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"A satellite is continuously pulled toward Earth by gravity.\nIts tangential velocity causes it to continuously move forward.\nGravity provides the centripetal acceleration required to keep it in orbit.\nThus, the satellite is effectively in continuous free fall around Earth.","revisionPoints":"Gravity provides centripetal acceleration.\nOrbital motion depends on both gravitational attraction and tangential velocity.\nA satellite is not beyond Earth's gravitational influence.\nDifferent orbital velocities produce different orbital paths."},
{"no":82,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding India's economic growth:\nIndia's GDP growth rate has increased continuously during the last five years.\nIndia's per capita income growth rate has increased continuously during the last five years.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["GDP Growth","Per Capita Income","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Economic growth rates can fluctuate from year to year.\nGDP growth does not necessarily follow a continuously increasing trajectory.\nPer capita income growth can similarly vary depending on GDP growth and population growth.\nTherefore, neither statement represents a continuously increasing trend.","revisionPoints":"GDP growth rate → measures the change in economic output.\nPer capita income → income/output measured per person.\nGrowth rates can fluctuate because of domestic and global factors.\nA single year's growth rate should not be treated as a permanent trend."},
{"no":83,"text":"Which category of institutions has the highest share in agricultural and allied-sector credit disbursement in India?","A":"Commercial banks","B":"Regional Rural Banks","C":"Cooperative banks","D":"Microfinance institutions","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking & Monetary Policy","tags":["Agriculture Credit","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Commercial banks account for the largest share of institutional agricultural credit.\nThey provide credit through various agricultural and allied-sector lending programmes.\nRegional Rural Banks and cooperative institutions also play important roles.\nInstitutional credit helps reduce dependence on informal sources of finance.","revisionPoints":"Agricultural credit → finances crop production and allied activities.\nCommercial banks are major institutional lenders.\nRegional Rural Banks focus strongly on rural and agricultural credit.\nCooperative banks also have an important role in rural finance."},
{"no":84,"text":"Which of the following can contribute to inclusive growth in India?\nSelf-Help Groups\nMicro, Small and Medium Enterprises\nRight to Education Act\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Inclusive Growth","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"SHGs can improve access to credit, savings and livelihood opportunities.\nMSMEs generate employment and support entrepreneurship.\nThe Right to Education promotes access to elementary education.\nTogether, these measures can expand participation in economic and social development.","revisionPoints":"Inclusive growth → benefits should reach different sections of society.\nSHGs → financial inclusion and livelihood support.\nMSMEs → employment and entrepreneurship.\nEducation → improves human capital and social mobility."},
{"no":85,"text":"With reference to disinvestment of Central Public Sector Enterprises (CPSEs), consider the following statements:\nThe main purpose of disinvestment is to repay India's external debt.\nAfter disinvestment, the Government necessarily loses all management control over the enterprise.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Disinvestment","CPSEs","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Disinvestment involves selling or reducing government ownership in a public-sector enterprise.\nIts objectives can include resource mobilisation, improving efficiency and broadening ownership.\nDisinvestment does not necessarily mean complete loss of government control.\nIt is also not primarily or exclusively intended to repay external debt.","revisionPoints":"Disinvestment → reduction of government equity ownership.\nStrategic disinvestment can involve transfer of management control.\nMinority disinvestment may retain government control.\nDisinvestment policy can be linked to fiscal and efficiency objectives."},
{"no":86,"text":"Consider the following statements about asteroids and comets:\nAsteroids are generally rocky or metallic bodies.\nComets are composed entirely of rocks and metals.\nA large number of asteroids are found between Mars and Jupiter.\nComets can develop a visible tail when they approach the Sun.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"1 and 3 only","C":"2 and 4 only","D":"1, 3 and 4 only","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Space","tags":["Asteroids","Comets","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Asteroids are primarily composed of rocky and metallic material.\nMany asteroids occupy the main asteroid belt between Mars and Jupiter.\nComets contain substantial amounts of ice, dust and rock, so statement 2 is incorrect.\nA comet can develop a tail when solar heating causes volatile material to escape, but the source's answer identifies statements 1 and 3.","revisionPoints":"Asteroid belt → mainly between Mars and Jupiter.\nComets → contain ice, dust and rocky material.\nComet tail generally becomes prominent near the Sun.\nAsteroids and comets are remnants from the early Solar System."},
{"no":87,"text":"Economic growth is usually accompanied by:","A":"Deflation","B":"Inflation","C":"Permanent unemployment","D":"Falling production","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Economic Growth","Inflation","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Economic expansion can increase aggregate demand.\nWhen demand rises faster than the economy's productive capacity, prices can increase.\nGrowth can therefore be associated with inflationary pressures.\nHowever, the relationship is not mechanically identical in every economic situation.","revisionPoints":"Economic growth → increase in real output.\nInflation → sustained increase in the general price level.\nDemand-pull inflation can occur when demand exceeds productive capacity.\nSupply-side conditions also influence inflation."},
{"no":88,"text":"A reduction in the Bank Rate is generally expected to:","A":"Increase liquidity in the economy","B":"Reduce liquidity in the economy","C":"Have no effect on credit conditions","D":"Increase taxation","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking & Monetary Policy","tags":["Bank Rate","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Bank Rate is a policy rate associated with the central bank's lending framework.\nA reduction in the rate can make borrowing conditions easier.\nEasier credit conditions can encourage banks to expand lending.\nIncreased credit availability can contribute to higher liquidity and economic activity.","revisionPoints":"Bank Rate → an important monetary-policy instrument.\nLower interest rates can encourage borrowing.\nHigher interest rates can restrain credit demand.\nMonetary policy influences liquidity, credit and inflation."},
{"no":89,"text":"Why are the westerlies generally stronger in the Southern Hemisphere?\nThe Southern Hemisphere has a larger proportion of oceans and less landmass.\nThe Coriolis force is stronger in the Southern Hemisphere.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Westerlies","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"The Southern Hemisphere has much more oceanic surface than the Northern Hemisphere.\nFewer large landmasses means less frictional disruption of the prevailing westerlies.\nCoriolis force is not inherently stronger merely because a location is in the Southern Hemisphere.\nTherefore, statement 2 is incorrect.","revisionPoints":"Westerlies → prevailing winds of the mid-latitudes.\nSouthern Hemisphere → larger oceanic area.\nOcean surfaces generally create less friction than land.\nCoriolis effect changes the direction of moving air but does not simply become stronger because of the hemisphere."},
{"no":90,"text":"Opening which of the following would significantly reduce navigation time and distance between India and East Asia?","A":"Panama Canal","B":"Kra Isthmus Canal","C":"Suez Canal","D":"Kiel Canal","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Malacca Straits","Kra Isthmus","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"The Kra Isthmus lies in southern Thailand, between the Andaman Sea and the Gulf of Thailand.\nA canal across the isthmus would provide an alternative route between the Indian Ocean and South China Sea.\nThis could reduce dependence on the longer route through the Strait of Malacca.","revisionPoints":"Kra Isthmus → located in Thailand.\nConnects the geographic regions of the Andaman Sea and Gulf of Thailand.\nStrait of Malacca → major existing maritime chokepoint.\nMaritime routes are important for India–East Asia trade."},
{"no":91,"text":"What is the principal role of antioxidants in the human body?","A":"Increase free-radical production","B":"Increase blood glucose","C":"Neutralize free radicals","D":"Prevent all infections","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Antioxidants","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Free radicals are highly reactive molecules that can cause oxidative stress.\nAntioxidants can neutralize or limit the damaging effects of certain reactive molecules.\nThey can therefore help protect cells from oxidative damage.\nAntioxidants do not prevent every type of infection.","revisionPoints":"Free radicals → highly reactive chemical species.\nExcessive free-radical activity can contribute to oxidative stress.\nAntioxidants help neutralize reactive species.\nSources include various fruits, vegetables and other foods."},
{"no":92,"text":"Consider the following statements about the Indus Valley Civilization:\nIt was predominantly secular in character.\nCotton was used for textile production.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Indus Valley Civilization","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"The archaeological record shows evidence of urban planning, trade, crafts and civic infrastructure.\nThe civilization is generally described as having a predominantly secular material culture in the context of this question.\nCotton was cultivated and used for making textiles.\nTextile production was an important craft activity.","revisionPoints":"Indus Valley Civilization → major Bronze Age urban civilization.\nImportant sites → Harappa, Mohenjo-daro, Dholavira, Lothal.\nEvidence of cotton textiles is significant.\nUrban features included drainage systems, planned streets and craft production."},
{"no":93,"text":"Which combination of crops is most suitable for the lower Gangetic plain, considering its humid climate and high temperatures?","A":"Wheat and gram","B":"Maize and barley","C":"Paddy and jute","D":"Cotton and groundnut","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Lower Gangetic Plain","Crops","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"The lower Gangetic plain has a humid climate, high temperatures and abundant water availability.\nPaddy is well adapted to warm and water-rich conditions.\nJute also requires a warm, humid environment and adequate moisture.\nTherefore, paddy and jute are particularly suitable.","revisionPoints":"Lower Gangetic plain → warm and humid.\nPaddy → requires substantial water and warm conditions.\nJute → important fibre crop suited to humid environments.\nCrop patterns are strongly influenced by climate, soil and water availability."},
{"no":94,"text":"The extensive belt of deserts in Africa and Eurasia is primarily associated with:","A":"Subtropical high-pressure belts","B":"Equatorial low pressure","C":"Warm ocean currents","D":"Polar easterlies alone","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Desert Belt","Sub-tropical High Pressure","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Around the subtropics, descending air associated with the Hadley circulation produces high-pressure conditions.\nDescending air is generally dry and inhibits cloud formation and rainfall.\nThis contributes to the formation of major subtropical deserts.\nExamples include the Sahara and Arabian deserts.","revisionPoints":"Subtropical high-pressure belt → roughly around 20°–30° latitude.\nDescending air → inhibits cloud formation and precipitation.\nMajor deserts occur in both hemispheres around subtropical latitudes.\nHadley circulation is important in understanding global pressure belts."},
{"no":95,"text":"Why do commercial aircraft generally fly in the lower stratosphere?\nThere is very little cloud formation and water vapour.\nThere is comparatively little vertical air movement.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Stratosphere","Jet Aircraft","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"The lower stratosphere is relatively stable compared with the troposphere.\nIt contains little water vapour and has few clouds.\nReduced vertical turbulence can provide smoother flight conditions.\nAircraft can therefore operate efficiently at these altitudes.","revisionPoints":"Troposphere → most weather phenomena occur here.\nStratosphere → comparatively stable atmospheric layer.\nCommercial aircraft often cruise near the tropopause/lower stratosphere.\nThe stratosphere contains the major part of the ozone layer."},
{"no":96,"text":"Biodiversity generally tends to be:\nGreater at lower latitudes.\nGreater at lower mountain altitudes.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Latitudinal Gradient","Biodiversity","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Species richness generally increases toward lower latitudes, producing the well-known latitudinal diversity gradient.\nAt higher mountain elevations, environmental conditions generally become colder and harsher.\nSpecies richness therefore often declines with increasing altitude.\nMany tropical regions consequently contain exceptionally high biodiversity.","revisionPoints":"Latitudinal diversity gradient → biodiversity generally increases toward the tropics.\nAltitudinal gradient → species richness often decreases with elevation.\nClimate, productivity and evolutionary history influence biodiversity patterns.\nTropical ecosystems contain a particularly high proportion of global biodiversity."},
{"no":97,"text":"The Brahmaputra River makes a pronounced U-turn around the eastern Himalayas mainly because of:","A":"Glacial erosion","B":"Syntaxial bending of the young Himalayas","C":"Volcanic activity","D":"Tidal action from the Bay of Bengal","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Brahmaputra","Syntaxial Bend","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Near the eastern end of the Himalayas, the mountain system undergoes a major syntaxial bend.\nThe Brahmaputra follows this structural configuration and makes a dramatic turn.\nThe river subsequently flows through the deep Himalayan landscape before entering the plains.","revisionPoints":"Brahmaputra → known as Tsangpo/Yarlung Tsangpo in Tibet.\nIt enters India through Arunachal Pradesh.\nThe eastern Himalayas show a major structural bend.\nThe river eventually enters the Assam valley and flows toward Bangladesh."},
{"no":98,"text":"Which Indian State is characterised by an arid/semi-arid northern region, a major cotton-producing central region and dominance of cash crops?","A":"Maharashtra","B":"Gujarat","C":"West Bengal","D":"Kerala","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Economic Geography","tags":["Gujarat","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Gujarat contains arid and semi-arid regions in its northern and western parts.\nIts black-soil regions support significant cotton cultivation.\nCommercial agriculture and cash crops are important components of the State's agricultural economy.\nTherefore, Gujarat best matches the description.","revisionPoints":"Gujarat → major cotton-producing State.\nBlack soils are particularly suitable for cotton.\nSaurashtra and Kutch have relatively dry climatic conditions.\nGujarat is also important for groundnut and other commercial crops."},
{"no":99,"text":"What is a Virtual Private Network (VPN)?","A":"A network that operates only without encryption","B":"A network that uses the public internet to provide secure access to an organisation's private network","C":"A network used exclusively for satellite communication","D":"A private physical cable connecting two offices","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"IT & Computers","tags":["Virtual Private Network","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"A VPN creates a protected communication connection over an existing network such as the public internet.\nIt can use encryption and authentication to protect transmitted data.\nOrganisations use VPNs to allow authorised users to securely access internal resources remotely.\nVPNs can also be used to establish secure connections between networks.","revisionPoints":"VPN → Virtual Private Network.\nUses network infrastructure to establish a protected connection.\nEncryption helps protect data in transit.\nCommon applications → remote work and secure network access."},
{"no":100,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nDharma refers to obligations and duties.\nRita (Rta) refers to the fundamental moral/cosmic order governing the universe.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Dharma and Rita","Vedic Civilization","UPSC","2011"],"expl":"Dharma has a broad meaning that includes duty, righteousness, moral order and appropriate conduct.\nRta (Rita) in Vedic thought refers to the underlying cosmic and moral order of the universe.\nBoth concepts are important in understanding the development of Indian philosophical and religious traditions.","revisionPoints":"Dharma → duty, righteousness and moral order.\nRta/Rita → Vedic concept of cosmic order.\nRta is associated with the orderly functioning of the universe.\nLater Indian traditions developed and expanded the concept of Dharma."},
];
